WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:05.960
Christian is beside me, stretched out on a
sun lounge. My husband – my hot, beautiful

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:11.269
husband, shirtless and in cut off jeans – is
reading a book predicting the collapse of

00:00:11.269 --> 00:00:14.230
the Western banking system.

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:21.819
“Me neither.” He grins, puts down his
BlackBerry, and stands. His shorts fall a

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:28.320
little and hang… in that way so his swim
trunks are visible beneath.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.040
Mister Grey will see you now.

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:36.980
[Title Music]

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:42.240
Fifty Shades Freed is a 2018 film directed
by James Foley, written by Niall Leonard adapted

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:46.420
from the 2012 novel of the same name written
by television executive Erika Mitchell under

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:48.700
the pen name E L James.

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:53.989
Originally published online as Twilight fanficion
between August 2010 and May 2011 under the

00:00:53.989 --> 00:00:59.120
title "Master of the Universe 2", Freed is
unique in the series in that it is the only

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:05.320
chunk of the trilogy that was somewhat conceived
as a finite story with publication in mind.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:10.430
By January of 2011, about half way through
"Master of the Universe 2"’s writing, Mitchell

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:15.090
had reached an agreement with Australian publisher
The Writer’s Coffee Shop and the process

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:20.031
of editing "Master of the Universe Part 1"
into Fifty Shades of Grey and Fifty Shades

00:01:20.031 --> 00:01:22.020
Darker had begun.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.990
This would be publicly announced at the end
of the March 5th instalment of "Master of

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:31.200
the Universe 2" in conjunction with the online
versions of more or less everything up to

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:36.600
that point being pulled from fanfiction.net
and fiftyshades.com.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.140
I have some exciting news – well exciting
for me.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:46.110
I have been given the opportunity to publish
this beast by TWCS. So I am going ahead and

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:47.210
doing that.

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:51.050
The first book... yes there’s more than
one – it’s sooo long as you know, will

00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:52.399
be out at the end of May.

00:01:52.399 --> 00:01:56.329
I have pulled every thing except the last
few chapters of motu2.

00:01:56.329 --> 00:02:00.880
I know a great deal of you will be delighted
for me... and some less so.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:04.299
You must tell me what you want... if you are
still speaking to me.

00:02:04.299 --> 00:02:09.050
I was actually able to find a copy of Master
of the Universe 2 with the blog notes still

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:15.100
intact, which makes for a fascinating read,
injecting the context of public performance

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:16.440
back into the text.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:22.690
Like, it’s wild to see that at the exact
moment publication was assured every outstanding

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:26.160
plot thread is either dropped or resolved.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:31.340
The entire main plot is resolved in the next
chapter after the announcement, and the three

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:36.930
after that are mostly various characters explaining
things to Bella and backfilling the majority

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:38.750
of the plot holes.

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:42.930
And you’ll be happy to know that the legacy
of Fifty Shades Freed as a hastily written

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:45.770
fill in has been preserved in the film version.

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:50.540
Sadly, they didn’t preserve the parts where
the American characters talk like a middle

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:52.340
aged English woman.

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:56.590
He smirks. “Eat,” he orders. “You didn’t
eat yesterday.”

00:02:56.590 --> 00:02:58.670
Oh, bossy Fifty!

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:01.670
“That’s because you were being an arse.”

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:05.600
Mrs. Jones drops something that clatters into
the sink, making me jump.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:09.590
Christian seems oblivious to the noise. Ignoring
her, he stares at me impassively.

00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:15.410
“Arse or not – eat.” his tone is serious.
No arguing with him.

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:20.450
Now, the main history of Fifty Shades that
I relayed in the first video focused entirely

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:25.370
on the pedigree of the films, the chain of
adaptation, but let’s take a minute to talk

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:27.740
about the parallel history to that.

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:32.620
In early 2012, as the deal with Vintage was
materializing, Mitchell tried to erase the

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:35.290
history of Fifty Shades from the internet.

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:40.680
The remaining content on FiftyShades.com was
deleted and replaced with a stock author bio

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:46.150
light blog, takedown notifications were issued
to various blogs that has syndicated the fanfic,

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:51.120
and Erika’s lawyers contacted the Internet
Archive and had both Fifty Shades.com and

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:56.040
the relevant pages on fanfiction.net removed
from the archive listing.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:00.130
Not only did this eliminate the most obvious
copies of the fic, but effectively wiped out

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:05.780
a tremendous amount of the parallel discussions
that are now left to only be relayed by word

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:07.580
of mouth.

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:14.140
Assembling the timeline of basic events like
“when was the first chapter posted to fanfiction.net?”

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:18.890
or “when were the books first published?”
was surprisingly difficult for something that

00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:21.290
happened only eight years ago.

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:27.710
The information is crumbling, some lost to
digital decay, and some to deliberate obfuscation.

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:35.120
The history of Fifty Shades has effectively
become folklore, and folklore is a delicate thing.

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:39.789
In interviews following publication Mitchell
would tell a few different variant stories

00:04:39.789 --> 00:04:44.900
about the origin of Fifty Shades, generally
downplaying or outright denying the fanfiction

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:46.650
origins of the story.

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:52.199
Even up through 2017 the go-to narrative was
this muddled sort of half truth about how

00:04:52.199 --> 00:04:58.969
she wrote a Twilight fanfiction and then wrote
another story that would go on to inspire

00:04:58.969 --> 00:05:00.080
Fifty Shades of Grey.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:05.100
I read about something called fan fiction
and I didn't know what fan fiction was and

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:10.400
I thought "well this is interesting" and I
looked up and um I decided to have a go myself

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:14.669
So I wrote, I wrote a novel, a seventy thousand
word novel in three weeks, and while I was

00:05:14.669 --> 00:05:18.330
doing that I had the idea for what became
Fifty Shades.

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:24.390
Now, it’s sort of a weird half truth because
there is another fanfic. Before Fifty Shades

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:30.360
of Grey, before Master of the Universe, there
was Safe Haven by SnowQueens IceDragon.

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:35.419
Edward Cullen is filming his new movie in
Las Vegas. His only solace is Bella, who lives

00:05:35.419 --> 00:05:42.310
and works in the luxury hotel he is staying.
But is she all she seems EXPLICIT adult content.

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:48.129
It’s not entirely clear which strictly came
first, but Safe Haven was the first fanfiction

00:05:48.129 --> 00:05:52.439
posted to Fanfiction.net under the handle
SnowQueens IceDragon,

00:05:52.439 --> 00:05:57.860
and it is functionally a one hundred and forty
three page prototype of Master of the Universe

00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:00.280
told from Edward’s perspective.

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:05.020
The obsession with Audi, the classical music,
the way Bella is a virgin who has never so

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:10.099
much as had an impure thought before Edward
comes along, the pervasive misogyny, and the

00:06:10.099 --> 00:06:15.580
fact that it’s really just a loose re-telling
of Twilight hitting most of the same major plot beats.

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:19.520
It’s revealing, because it sets out patterns
in Mitchell’s writing that can be seen repeating

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:24.810
throughout the rest of her work, elements
that remain fixed in place even after edits

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:32.020
or adaptations, the critical one being this:
Anna is not the self-insert of Fifty Shades,

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:33.740
Christian is.

00:06:33.749 --> 00:06:38.969
This is part of why I find Safe Haven fascinating.
The first fanfic wasn't Bella's point of view

00:06:38.969 --> 00:06:45.140
it was Edward’s, an Edward that is controlling,
demanding, petty, and easily angered.

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:50.159
Through adaptations, though mutation from
Edward to Christian, these are the personality

00:06:50.159 --> 00:06:52.000
traits that stay intact.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:57.939
The screenwriter is replaced and given a mandate
to preserve the accuracy of the books, and

00:06:57.939 --> 00:07:00.469
what’s the thing that survives the process?

00:07:00.469 --> 00:07:04.969
Dumbass arguments about an email address,
where Christian uses his power to get pointless

00:07:04.969 --> 00:07:10.919
concessions out of people in a way that improves
nothing, but makes him happy because now people

00:07:10.919 --> 00:07:12.300
are doing his bidding.

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:15.510
I tried emailing you. It bounced.

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:19.460
We talked a bunch in the last video about
how Erika pretty much hijacked the production,

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:22.259
but I didn’t really talk about how.

00:07:22.259 --> 00:07:27.240
The leverage that she kept using on the set
of the first film was that if any change displeased

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:33.319
her too much she would withdraw support and
tell all her fans to boycott the movie, which,

00:07:33.319 --> 00:07:37.099
in the context of a film like Fifty Shades
is pretty much death.

00:07:37.099 --> 00:07:42.340
It’s the kind of movie that relies very
heavily on fans to be the backbone, to be

00:07:42.340 --> 00:07:48.770
the word of mouth, more than on a general
audience just going to see it because it looks cool.

00:07:48.770 --> 00:07:55.719
Marketing wise it was 100% banking on being
the movie version of a cultural phenomenon

00:07:55.719 --> 00:07:59.210
as opposed to the adaptation of a good story.

00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:02.650
With this in mind it ends up being really
fascinating when you watch the behind the

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:08.050
scenes extras for Darker and the selling point
for the new writer and director, Niall Leonard

00:08:08.050 --> 00:08:12.069
and James Foley, is “they get along with
Erika.”

00:08:12.069 --> 00:08:17.900
He's incredibly experienced and he has a,
has a very impressive roster of great films

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:22.020
under him. And we met and we just hit it off.

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:25.960
And Niall, who's actually Erika's husband,
did the adaptation, which I thought was a

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:30.500
good thing for us as producers because he
lived with these books and the process of

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:31.849
these books being written.

00:08:31.849 --> 00:08:40.060
He did a really fantastic job of boiling everything
down so that we got all the main set pieces

00:08:40.060 --> 00:08:43.500
and fan moments that would be expected.

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:47.000
He kinda went off and did his thing and then
he'd bring me a draft and say "what do you

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:51.020
think?" and I'd go "oh this this this" and
we're still speaking to each other so I think

00:08:51.020 --> 00:08:52.510
that's probably a good thing.

00:08:52.510 --> 00:08:56.570
And just from watching the on-set interactions
in all this material and how Erika is pretty

00:08:56.570 --> 00:09:02.460
much just directing the movie it’s obvious
that Mitchell threatened Universal that she

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:07.810
would take her ball and go home, so they found
her a proxy writer in her husband, and a proxy

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:09.460
director in James Foley.

00:09:09.460 --> 00:09:13.900
It’s also fascinating how Grey’s behind
the scenes stuff spends a lot of time talking

00:09:13.900 --> 00:09:19.150
with Johnson and Dornan about the sex scenes,
and both of them relay at length how much

00:09:19.150 --> 00:09:25.060
trust they put in director Sam Taylor-Johnson,
how much work she put into helping them find

00:09:25.060 --> 00:09:29.890
a head space they could be in and work in,
and then for the second and third movies they’re

00:09:29.890 --> 00:09:34.210
out on the press junket talking about doing
whisky shots to get through the sex scenes.

00:09:34.210 --> 00:09:36.960
Do you guys have a pre-game ritual before
these scenes?

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:44.670
Shot of whisky, and, yeah. He does push ups
and I just lay there and drink whisky.

00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:48.750
On the lighter side of that subject, I’m
pretty sure I can identify the first scene

00:09:48.750 --> 00:09:53.530
where Dakota Johnson was drinking to get through
the day, and it’s the topless boat scene

00:09:53.530 --> 00:09:59.380
from Darker. The tell to me is that Dakota
Johnson just seems kinda drunk.

00:09:59.380 --> 00:10:02.630
Who did this to you?

00:10:02.630 --> 00:10:05.511
So one of the things that really stood out
to me as I was getting into all of this, as

00:10:05.511 --> 00:10:10.750
I was researching and assembling this whole
long project, was that with every pass I made

00:10:10.750 --> 00:10:15.970
over the movies the differences became more
and more clear. The level of craft put into

00:10:15.970 --> 00:10:20.440
the smaller things became more and more identifiable.

00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:25.510
This is how e first see him. Very structured,
very businesslike.

00:10:25.510 --> 00:10:32.080
The next he takes her on this small coffee
date. It's still guarded, with the vest, protecting

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:38.571
the heart area, but it's a little more open,
and trying to be more of a regular guy. It's

00:10:38.571 --> 00:10:41.160
also worn with blue jeans.

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:47.880
It's open, it's one level, it's a different
colour, and has a little flare with the pocket

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:54.870
square. And so in subtle ways we find him
opening up and wrapping his mind around becoming

00:10:54.870 --> 00:10:56.900
part of her life.

00:10:56.900 --> 00:11:00.840
Erika was very keen that movies two and three
should stick as closely as possible to the

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:04.500
books and to the sense of the books and to
the sense of the story.

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:09.170
Freed is basically a low budget James Bond
knockoff, like if instead of having a whole

00:11:09.170 --> 00:11:13.760
bunch of wild gadgets, a car chase, a boat
chase, and a fight on a helicopter there was

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:20.120
instead a gadget, a car going over the speed
limit, a boat, and a fight.

00:11:20.120 --> 00:11:24.450
Anyway, the movie opens with a wedding montage
and already I want to stop and talk about

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:28.090
the sickly green under-tone that’s been
graded into this scene.

00:11:28.090 --> 00:11:34.611
So, green is very common in nature, but it’s
rare for the ambient light to be green. Green

00:11:34.611 --> 00:11:39.820
ambiance is unnatural and off-putting, so
using green as an undertone, or even as an

00:11:39.820 --> 00:11:46.570
overtone, is, in cinematic language, shorthand
for a state that is unnatural, disconcerting,

00:11:46.570 --> 00:11:51.510
or wrong. It’s why The Matrix is tinted
green, why the office in Joe Versus the Volcano

00:11:51.510 --> 00:11:56.470
is green, and why the first Twilight movie
is pretty much entirely green until Bella

00:11:56.470 --> 00:11:59.000
decides to stay in Forks.

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:03.580
Also the fact that it is unnatural makes it
a really obvious tell. If you’re watching

00:12:03.580 --> 00:12:08.250
a movie and the lighting looks tinted green
it’s because someone was mucking with it,

00:12:08.250 --> 00:12:11.670
it didn’t just happen to look like that.

00:12:11.670 --> 00:12:14.950
Given that Freed is going to be more of a
thriller it makes sense that we’re going

00:12:14.950 --> 00:12:18.880
to see green used as an undertone, and it
makes sense that it would appear early in

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:20.690
the film to set the mood.

00:12:20.690 --> 00:12:24.950
It makes sense when Jack Hyde is breaking
into Grey Enterprises Holdings Incorporated

00:12:24.950 --> 00:12:30.620
to plan a fire bomb in the server room, but
it doesn’t make sense for it to be used

00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:36.980
in a wedding scene when that wedding is supposed
to be fairy tale beautiful, when this is supposed

00:12:36.980 --> 00:12:42.760
to be the emotional high before anything has
had a chance to spoil it.

00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:48.850
And as a visual shorthand its use is really
inconsistent. Some relevant scenes have it,

00:12:48.850 --> 00:12:53.880
some don’t, and there’s nowhere else in
the movie where it’s as pervasive as it

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:54.880
is in the wedding.

00:12:54.880 --> 00:13:00.900
Alright, so, we’re thirty seconds into the
film and I’ve already gone on for a minute about colour. Cool.

00:13:00.900 --> 00:13:05.490
Anyway, the rest of the title montage is all
honeymoon stuff in Paris and things finally

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:08.870
settle on the beach with Ana asking Christian
to apply more sunscreen.

00:13:08.870 --> 00:13:10.340
Could you put this on my back?

00:13:10.340 --> 00:13:11.380
Take off the whole thing.

00:13:11.380 --> 00:13:13.160
Not a chance. you're showing plenty as it
is.

00:13:13.160 --> 00:13:15.640
I am wearing more than any woman here.

00:13:15.640 --> 00:13:19.060
You want to be ogled by every guy on the beach?

00:13:19.060 --> 00:13:23.760
To my disappointment they decided to skip
on the literal adaptation of Christian Grey,

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:29.290
image conscious billionaire, lounging at the
beach in cutoff jeans over swim trunks.

00:13:29.290 --> 00:13:30.640
Ladies.

00:13:30.640 --> 00:13:35.340
Christian leaves to have a swim, and Ana removes
the rest of her top anyway.

00:13:35.340 --> 00:13:38.630
Cutting away from that, a mysterious stranger
gets into Christian’s office tower, changes

00:13:38.630 --> 00:13:43.060
into a disguise, hacks the electronic lock
on the server room, and then plants a bomb.

00:13:43.060 --> 00:13:44.060
Wait for it.

00:13:44.060 --> 00:13:48.260
Christian and Ana argue again about Ana taking
her top off, and Christian finally reminds

00:13:48.260 --> 00:13:52.780
her that there might be paparazzi around,
continuing the fine tradition of Christian

00:13:52.780 --> 00:13:57.590
always being justified for pushing Ana around.
See, it’s just like when he pulled her out

00:13:57.590 --> 00:14:00.000
of the way of the bike.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:02.760
Then the argument ends and they go back to
the boat and have sex.

00:14:02.760 --> 00:14:08.140
Christian’s assistant Ros calls and informs
them that the bomb did indeed, finally, go off.

00:14:08.140 --> 00:14:16.760
It totally exploded, uh, you know, over there.
Off screen. Just made a real mess of things.

00:14:16.770 --> 00:14:23.780
Totally fried the servers. Lots of damage.
Really expensive damage. Just over, you know,

00:14:23.780 --> 00:14:27.970
over there.

00:14:27.970 --> 00:14:29.140
Anyway it’s Jack Hyde.

00:14:29.140 --> 00:14:31.440
Oh my god, that's Jack.

00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:35.870
Oh and also the helicopter crash was totally
sabotage and that was also Jack Hyde.

00:14:35.870 --> 00:14:40.380
I got a report from the FAA, they don't think
that the helicopter crash was an accident.

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:42.550
they said it looks like sabotage.

00:14:42.550 --> 00:14:46.300
Jack Hyde, fiction editor, sabotaged Christian’s
helicopter.

00:14:46.300 --> 00:14:51.650
The day after Jack Hyde, fiction editor, was
fired from his job as fiction editor, Jack

00:14:51.650 --> 00:14:54.400
Hyde, fiction editor, sabotaged a helicopter.

00:14:54.400 --> 00:14:58.740
Ana and Christian cut their honeymoon short,
go home, meet the new security guards Christian

00:14:58.740 --> 00:15:03.170
has hired, and get into a petty argument about
having children, starting one of the major

00:15:03.170 --> 00:15:04.670
threads for the movie.

00:15:04.670 --> 00:15:07.410
Don’t worry, it gets worse.

00:15:07.410 --> 00:15:11.000
Ana goes to work where she finds out that
while she was gone on her honeymoon her temporary

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:16.420
promotion has been made permanent and the
office was renovated and Liz is pretty obviously

00:15:16.420 --> 00:15:18.340
resentful of the whole situation.

00:15:18.340 --> 00:15:20.910
I mean, yeah.

00:15:20.910 --> 00:15:26.040
By the way, Liz is the head of human resources
at SIP, who you might remember from the last

00:15:26.040 --> 00:15:32.260
movie as the woman who clearly knew Jack was
bad news but still encouraged Ana to spend

00:15:32.260 --> 00:15:34.550
time with him in a social capacity.

00:15:34.550 --> 00:15:39.171
Ana's assistant Hannah comes in and the two
talk about changing Ana’s email address

00:15:39.171 --> 00:15:45.820
from Anastasia Steel to Anastasia Grey and
Ana just says you know what, leave it for now.

00:15:45.820 --> 00:15:49.960
The scene wraps up and cuts to Ana taking
a meeting with her biggest client, Boyce Fox,

00:15:49.960 --> 00:15:55.410
when Christian comes storming into the office,
demands to speak with Ana immediately, insinuates

00:15:55.410 --> 00:16:00.630
that she’s cheating with her client, then
throws a fit because he tried to send an email

00:16:00.630 --> 00:16:05.680
to Anastasia Grey at publishing company dot
com and it bounced

00:16:05.680 --> 00:16:12.090
I tried email you. It bounced. There's no
Anastasia Grey at SIP.

00:16:12.090 --> 00:16:13.740
and we’re going to talk about lighting.

00:16:13.740 --> 00:16:18.540
Now, this whole point is incredibly petty,
but the fact that they didn’t re-light the

00:16:18.540 --> 00:16:23.080
set between the two scenes is what really
elevates it to comedy for me.

00:16:23.080 --> 00:16:27.340
It isn’t just later the same day, it’s
basically immediately.

00:16:27.340 --> 00:16:31.830
Ana goes to work, the sun streaming through
her windows, and before she’s even taken

00:16:31.830 --> 00:16:36.400
a coffee break Christian is standing in her
office demanding to know if their marriage

00:16:36.400 --> 00:16:41.370
is a sham because she has been back to work
for forty five minutes already and hasn’t

00:16:41.370 --> 00:16:44.500
yet changed her email address!

00:16:44.500 --> 00:16:50.500
To this compelling argument Ana caves pretty
much instantly because she has at this point

00:16:50.500 --> 00:16:54.540
more or less accepted that her life largely
consists of managing Christian’s emotions

00:16:54.540 --> 00:17:00.620
for him, and his face lights up with just
the most punchable grin.

00:17:00.620 --> 00:17:05.409
Just a reminder, this isn’t even an argument
about her changing her legal name, it’s

00:17:05.409 --> 00:17:11.910
purely about her changing her professional
name and her email address.

00:17:11.910 --> 00:17:14.419
Also it will never come up again.

00:17:14.419 --> 00:17:19.159
There’s no future complication as a result
of changing email addresses and it doesn’t

00:17:19.159 --> 00:17:22.459
even become petty ammunition in a future argument.

00:17:22.459 --> 00:17:27.220
Ana says she’ll think about it, Christian’s
like “yay, I bullied my wife into giving

00:17:27.220 --> 00:17:32.940
me a petty win” and just like the bathing
suit it’s never mentioned again.

00:17:32.940 --> 00:17:37.149
After work Christian drives Ana out to the
country and surprises her with a house.

00:17:37.149 --> 00:17:41.010
I didn’t even mention this in the previous
movie, but when Christian and Ana spent a

00:17:41.010 --> 00:17:44.990
day on his boat Ana saw this house on the
shore and was like “that’s a nice house,

00:17:44.990 --> 00:17:48.790
I love that house” and now Christian is
like “hey I bought you this house that you

00:17:48.790 --> 00:17:52.309
like” and then Christian’s architect comes
in and is like “cool, so, we’re going

00:17:52.309 --> 00:17:56.110
to bulldoze the house” and Christian is
like “yes, let’s bulldoze the house that

00:17:56.110 --> 00:18:00.049
I bought for my wife because she said that
she loves the house.”

00:18:00.049 --> 00:18:04.830
If that sounds like bizarro whiplash that’s
because it’s setup for Ana’s transformation

00:18:04.830 --> 00:18:09.779
into a wealth monster, because the architect,
Gia, is a blonde, and as we’ve discussed

00:18:09.779 --> 00:18:14.519
blondes in Fifty Shades are all homewreckers
and temptresses, so Ana dresses her down for

00:18:14.520 --> 00:18:19.740
the vicarious enjoyment of Erika Mitchell
who hates women in general and blondes in specific.

00:18:19.740 --> 00:18:24.040
Oh, and this is a good place for a reminder
that in the logic of the story, Christian

00:18:24.050 --> 00:18:29.799
hired Gia specifically because she’s a blonde
so that she wouldn’t remind him of his dead

00:18:29.799 --> 00:18:34.950
mother, who he refers to as The Crack Whore,
and thus he wouldn’t feel compelled to beat

00:18:34.950 --> 00:18:38.169
her sexually in an act of displaced revenge.

00:18:38.169 --> 00:18:41.809
Anyway, Christian lets Ana drive home and
they get into a car chase and then have sex

00:18:41.809 --> 00:18:43.190
in a parking lot.

00:18:43.190 --> 00:18:47.340
Back home their security tells them the driver
that was chasing them is a woman, then Ana

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:53.200
finds Leila’s gun, still loaded, in Christian’s
desk drawer and he promises to get rid of it.

00:18:53.200 --> 00:18:55.519
I'll get rid of it.

00:18:55.519 --> 00:18:59.260
A whole bunch of nothing happens until Ana
goes out with Kate for drinks, even though

00:18:59.260 --> 00:19:03.490
Christian told her to go straight home, and
then Ana gets home and Jack Hyde, who has

00:19:03.490 --> 00:19:08.260
broken into their apartment, attacks her with
a kitchen knife and tries to kidnap her, but

00:19:08.260 --> 00:19:10.249
he’s taken down by her new security guards.

00:19:10.249 --> 00:19:11.620
Better restrain him.

00:19:11.620 --> 00:19:13.450
I don't have anything.

00:19:13.450 --> 00:19:17.570
We do. I mean, we, I can find something.

00:19:17.570 --> 00:19:24.419
Alright, that line is kinda funny. Like, it’s
funny on purpose. I like that line, it's funny on purpose.

00:19:24.419 --> 00:19:27.950
Also because this detail is hilarious I want
to point out that in the book this doesn’t

00:19:27.950 --> 00:19:31.249
happen, Jack doesn’t take Ana captive at
knife point.

00:19:31.249 --> 00:19:35.779
Instead Ana gets home from the bar and the
action is already resolved, Jack broke in

00:19:35.779 --> 00:19:40.740
while she was gone and her security team has
already incapacitated him.

00:19:40.740 --> 00:19:43.549
Everything exciting already happened off screen.

00:19:43.549 --> 00:19:50.800
See, that’s what happens when you don’t
actually edit your fanfic before publishing it as a novel.

00:19:50.800 --> 00:19:55.620
Ana is talking with the police who tell her
that in Jack’s stolen van they found a note

00:19:55.620 --> 00:20:01.940
saying “you owe me a life” and assure
her that he’s definitely not going to get out.

00:20:01.940 --> 00:20:05.940
Alright, then there’s three scenes where
nothing really happens, but Christian isn’t

00:20:05.950 --> 00:20:09.870
happy, then Ana gets home from work and Christian
takes her to the playroom where he tortures

00:20:09.870 --> 00:20:16.179
her with orgasm denial in revenge for saying
she would go home but then going out drinking

00:20:16.179 --> 00:20:21.490
with Kate, and it escalates to the point that
Ana needs to use her safe word.

00:20:21.490 --> 00:20:25.429
Now, okay, there’s a bit to unravel here.

00:20:25.429 --> 00:20:29.350
First I’m going to refer back to what I
said in the first part of their series. It’s

00:20:29.350 --> 00:20:35.030
okay, from a narrative perspective, if Christian
is a dick, it’s just that the conflict that

00:20:35.030 --> 00:20:38.490
arises out of it needs to go somewhere.

00:20:38.490 --> 00:20:43.271
The issue is that despite the fact that Ana
is the one who has been wronged here the scene

00:20:43.280 --> 00:20:51.860
still pivots to be about Christian’s manpain
and explain why it is that he just can’t stop abusing her.

00:20:51.860 --> 00:20:56.060
And with that violation of trust out of the
way the movie comes to a standstill for fifteen

00:20:56.070 --> 00:20:59.710
minutes while Ana and the gang piss off to
Aspen for the weekend.

00:20:59.710 --> 00:21:04.619
Wait, the bikini, the email address, the attempt
at punishing her for going out with Kate despite

00:21:04.619 --> 00:21:08.899
the fact that she was attacked at knife point
less than twenty four hours earlier, it’s

00:21:08.899 --> 00:21:13.200
almost like there’s a pattern of Christian
violating Ana’s boundaries then giving her

00:21:13.200 --> 00:21:17.619
extravagant gifts and/or sex in lieu of apologizing.

00:21:17.619 --> 00:21:21.799
When they get back Detective Clark tells Ana
that Jack Hyde, the man who was caught on

00:21:21.799 --> 00:21:27.519
camera planting a bomb, stole a vehicle, and
attempted to kidnap Ana at knifepoint, is

00:21:27.519 --> 00:21:33.049
probably going to get bail because he’s
saying that she came on to him first and he

00:21:33.049 --> 00:21:39.250
totally didn’t try to molest her, so extenuating
circumstances?

00:21:39.250 --> 00:21:43.029
Ana goes to the courthouse for his bail hearing
and sure enough he gets bail, though the judge

00:21:43.029 --> 00:21:47.559
sets bail at half a million dollars because
it’s his first offence.

00:21:47.559 --> 00:21:51.690
Or, well, his first slate of multiple offences
including criminal arson, grand theft auto,

00:21:51.690 --> 00:21:54.950
and attempted kidnapping. But who’s keeping
count.

00:21:54.950 --> 00:21:59.970
Ana immediately runs to the bathroom and throws
up, then hard cut to the doctor’s office

00:21:59.970 --> 00:22:04.389
where Ana’s gynecologist tells her that
whoopsie doodle she’s pregnant because she

00:22:04.389 --> 00:22:08.340
hasn’t taken her birth control since the
end of the last movie.

00:22:08.340 --> 00:22:12.289
At dinner Ana tells Christian that she’s
pregnant and Christian is like “that’s

00:22:12.289 --> 00:22:17.759
bad, I don’t want a baby right now” and
Ana is like “I know it’s bad, I don’t

00:22:17.759 --> 00:22:21.899
want a baby right now, either” and even
though that makes it sound like the two of

00:22:21.900 --> 00:22:28.620
them are totally on the same page vis a vis
this pregnancy, they just yell at each other instead.

00:22:28.620 --> 00:22:32.800
Christian storms off, eventually coming home
in the middle of the night, piss drunk, where

00:22:32.810 --> 00:22:37.289
he slurs about his fear that Ana will choose
the baby over him.

00:22:37.289 --> 00:22:42.200
As Ana is putting his stuff away he gets a
text from Elena saying “good to see you

00:22:42.200 --> 00:22:46.559
tonight, I’m here for you, anytime.” which
sets Ana off, so she goes and sleeps in the

00:22:46.559 --> 00:22:47.559
playroom.

00:22:47.559 --> 00:22:50.900
The next morning Christian is busy organizing
the help into a search and rescue party when

00:22:50.900 --> 00:22:56.750
Ana comes downstairs. This begins a prolonged
argument between the two where Ana is naked

00:22:56.750 --> 00:22:58.169
for most of it.

00:22:58.169 --> 00:23:05.520
Like, it’s just an entire scene of dialogue
and throughout Ana is just naked.

00:23:05.520 --> 00:23:11.090
It’s nudity that’s pointless to the point
it’s honestly kinda surreal.

00:23:11.090 --> 00:23:16.159
Alright, I don’t know how to segue this,
because just saying it out loud makes it sound

00:23:16.159 --> 00:23:22.010
somehow even more abrupt than it actually
is, but like an hour later Ana gets a phone

00:23:22.010 --> 00:23:23.059
call from Mia

00:23:23.059 --> 00:23:27.450
EXCEPT IT’S JACK HYDE AND HE WANTS FIVE
MILLION DOLLARS IN RANSOM IN TWO HOURS OR

00:23:27.450 --> 00:23:29.590
HE’S GOING TO KILL MIA.

00:23:29.590 --> 00:23:36.220
So… I don’t… I don’t know what Jack’s
exit strategy is here?

00:23:36.220 --> 00:23:44.169
He’s out on bail for a bunch of really serious
crimes, and Ana knows exactly who he is, so

00:23:44.169 --> 00:23:48.429
it’s not like he’s some anonymous dude
who can grab a duffle bag full of money and

00:23:48.429 --> 00:23:50.100
just disappear.

00:23:50.100 --> 00:23:54.580
And he doesn’t know this, but Christian
doesn’t actually like his family all that

00:23:54.580 --> 00:24:00.100
much, so killing Mia would maybe make Christian
a bit sad but mostly it’s just going to

00:24:00.100 --> 00:24:05.410
add murder to Jack’s already substantial
list of crimes that he’s already definitely

00:24:05.410 --> 00:24:07.080
going to prison for.

00:24:07.080 --> 00:24:10.619
I dunno, we’ll talk more about Jack’s
plan later.

00:24:10.619 --> 00:24:15.360
In the meantime Ana goes home, changes into
her teenage runaway outfit, gets a duffle

00:24:15.360 --> 00:24:19.480
bag, the chequebook, and grabs Leila’s gun,
which Christian had promised to get rid of

00:24:19.480 --> 00:24:20.559
several weeks ago.

00:24:20.559 --> 00:24:22.860
I'll get rid of it.

00:24:22.860 --> 00:24:28.929
Hey, isn’t it weird how whenever Ana breaks
tiny, inconsequential promises like “I’ll

00:24:28.929 --> 00:24:33.720
come straight home after work” or “I won’t
take my top off on a topless beach” it’s

00:24:33.720 --> 00:24:37.860
deemed worthy of derision and punishment,
but when Christian breaks his promise to get

00:24:37.860 --> 00:24:42.341
rid of a loaded handgun that he keeps in an
unlocked drawer in their home it’s fine

00:24:42.341 --> 00:24:45.659
because it totally turned out Ana needed a
loaded handgun?

00:24:45.660 --> 00:24:53.000
Anyway, now that she’s armed and dangerous
she gives Sawyer the slip and takes off for the bank.

00:24:53.460 --> 00:24:58.259
At the bank Ana, dressed in a hoodie and clearly
in a hurry, asks to withdraw five million

00:24:58.259 --> 00:25:00.840
dollars in cash from the joint account.

00:25:00.840 --> 00:25:05.020
Banks don’t carry that much cash on hand,
by the way, but just accept it or we’ll

00:25:05.020 --> 00:25:06.039
be here all day.

00:25:06.039 --> 00:25:10.269
The bank manager, Troy Whelan, calls Christian
to confirm the transaction, and Christian

00:25:10.269 --> 00:25:12.950
asks to speak to Ana in private.

00:25:12.950 --> 00:25:17.909
After their conversation a sobbing Ana gives
the phone back, and Christian tells Whelan

00:25:17.909 --> 00:25:19.830
to give her the money.

00:25:19.830 --> 00:25:25.399
Despite this being a situation built entirely
out of red flags, Whelan gives her the cash,

00:25:25.399 --> 00:25:29.970
and his phone, and helps her take the money
out the back door of the bank and load it

00:25:29.970 --> 00:25:35.850
into an SUV being driven by a person who looks
like they’re trying to look shady.

00:25:35.850 --> 00:25:41.120
Oh, and the driver is Liz. You know, the head
of human resources at SIP? She’s the one

00:25:41.120 --> 00:25:43.990
who got into the high speed car chase through
Seattle.

00:25:43.990 --> 00:25:47.509
So Christian has figured out that something
is up, and that Mia’s missing, so he calls

00:25:47.509 --> 00:25:52.160
the cops, and he’s tracking Ana’s cell
phone which she hid in the bag of money, so

00:25:52.160 --> 00:25:55.450
everyone’s converging on the spot where
Jack has Mia tied up.

00:25:55.450 --> 00:26:00.539
Jack beats Ana up and kicks her in the stomach,
so oh no pregnant, then Ana shoots Jack and

00:26:00.539 --> 00:26:03.160
the police arrive and that’s it.

00:26:03.160 --> 00:26:07.590
The entire standoff resolves in about 45 seconds.

00:26:07.590 --> 00:26:10.539
From there it’s just a slow roll downhill
to the end.

00:26:10.539 --> 00:26:15.350
Christian wants a baby now, and the baby’s
okay, and here’s all of Jack Hyde’s motivations

00:26:15.350 --> 00:26:17.860
and stuff sort of I guess.

00:26:17.860 --> 00:26:21.690
Christian is shocked because he realizes that
the only substantial difference between him

00:26:21.690 --> 00:26:26.009
and being a pervert middle manager is that
he started off with millions of dollars and

00:26:26.009 --> 00:26:31.820
turned it into billions, but this rare moment
of accurate self-assessment is undercut by Ana disagreeing.

00:26:31.820 --> 00:26:41.160
If I'd been left in the system who knows how
I would have turned out?

00:26:41.160 --> 00:26:44.440
You're a man of honour. And you treat people
well.

00:26:44.440 --> 00:26:46.510
I'll get rid of it.

00:26:46.510 --> 00:26:48.990
You're showing plenty as it is.

00:26:48.990 --> 00:26:52.100
I get off on punishing women, women who look
like you

00:26:52.100 --> 00:26:53.249
like your mother

00:26:53.249 --> 00:26:59.010
I tried emailing you. It bounced. There's
no Anastasia Grey at SIP.

00:26:59.010 --> 00:27:00.990
And you treat people well.

00:27:00.990 --> 00:27:04.879
Turns out Jack and Christian were in the same
foster home in Detroit and Jack remembers

00:27:04.879 --> 00:27:10.190
and resents Christian and we’ll talk about
this in a bit, we’re almost done the movie.

00:27:10.190 --> 00:27:14.070
Ana and Christian visit the gave of Christian’s
birth mother and he doesn’t once call her

00:27:14.070 --> 00:27:15.070
The Crack Whore.

00:27:15.070 --> 00:27:21.760
The crack whore used to let me play with her hair. I think. I don't know if it's a memory or a dream.

00:27:21.760 --> 00:27:23.370
Whoa! His birth mom.

00:27:23.370 --> 00:27:27.419
There’s a montage of all the highlights
from the trilogy, which is weird because it

00:27:27.419 --> 00:27:30.950
really just calls attention to the fact that
nothing has really happened.

00:27:30.950 --> 00:27:34.619
Then the movie fake ends with Ana setting
up the playroom, and it’s the first time

00:27:34.619 --> 00:27:39.669
in the movies that Christian’s ratty dom
jeans have gotten any explicit attention rather

00:27:39.669 --> 00:27:45.669
than just being an environmental detail, so
this loving close up as Ana reverently laying

00:27:45.669 --> 00:27:49.109
them out on the bed is kinda comical.

00:27:49.109 --> 00:27:53.600
Christian remarks that Ana is topping from
the bottom, a line we are going to be talking

00:27:53.600 --> 00:27:58.659
about a lot in a bit, and fade to black for
a few credits before an epilogue where everyone’s

00:27:58.659 --> 00:28:02.749
happy and rich and Ana’s pregnant with their
second kid.

00:28:02.749 --> 00:28:04.239
The.

00:28:04.239 --> 00:28:05.730
End.

00:28:05.730 --> 00:28:11.669
Alright, get a drink, we’ll be right back.

00:28:11.669 --> 00:28:20.149
This episode was brought to you by tap water.
Mm, mm, good old municipal tap water.

00:28:20.149 --> 00:28:24.850
This is, by far, the most literal adaptation
of the three, no doubt helped by Master of

00:28:24.850 --> 00:28:31.429
the Universe 2 being somewhat more self contained
and less of a sprawling catastrophe.

00:28:31.429 --> 00:28:35.950
There’s a bunch of stuff, but the main one
that I really want to talk about is that last

00:28:35.950 --> 00:28:40.889
line because I’ve been wanting to talk about
it since we started. It’s this line from

00:28:40.889 --> 00:28:44.840
Christian to Ana “I believe you’re topping
from the bottom.”

00:28:44.840 --> 00:28:46.179
Flashback time!

00:28:46.179 --> 00:28:51.119
Mitchell also has an awkward habit of juxtaposing
erotic imagery with infantilizing internal

00:28:51.119 --> 00:28:55.730
commentary from Anastasia. Ana is seemingly
perpetually confused by the world around her

00:28:55.730 --> 00:29:00.600
and naive to the point of farce, a thread
that persists all the way to the very end

00:29:00.600 --> 00:29:01.600
of the trilogy.

00:29:01.600 --> 00:29:06.029
“You know, you’re topping from the bottom,”
he murmurs against my lips.

00:29:06.029 --> 00:29:10.269
“What?” I don’t understand what he’s
talking about.

00:29:10.269 --> 00:29:13.360
If you’ve been following along with the
movies and you still find these characters

00:29:13.360 --> 00:29:19.090
to be insufferable and frustrating that’s
fair, but I promise you it doesn’t hold

00:29:19.090 --> 00:29:27.119
a candle to the books. Mitchell as a writer
is seemingly incapable of ever changing characterization.

00:29:27.119 --> 00:29:32.820
All the way up to the final pages of the book
Ana is still utterly oblivious to the world

00:29:32.820 --> 00:29:35.309
that she has gotten herself into.

00:29:35.309 --> 00:29:41.630
So this change, having Ana implicitly acknowledge
that she is, in fact, the one who’s really

00:29:41.630 --> 00:29:46.940
in control here, I like it. I wish it were
in a better movie.

00:29:46.940 --> 00:29:51.259
One of my favourite changes from the book,
mostly because of how it contrasts the original,

00:29:51.259 --> 00:29:54.600
has to be the ending with Christian’s mom’s
grave stone.

00:29:54.600 --> 00:29:59.429
Like, this is such a natural end point for
Christian as a character. The book is even

00:29:59.429 --> 00:30:06.509
called Fifty Shades Freed. Christian is Fifty
Shades. Ana calls him “Fifty” constantly

00:30:06.509 --> 00:30:12.519
in her inner monologue, so the title of the
book is Christian Grey, Freed. It is the book

00:30:12.519 --> 00:30:18.519
where, ostensibly, Christian finally gets
over his myriad problems and can be normal.

00:30:18.519 --> 00:30:21.169
Except, he doesn’t.

00:30:21.169 --> 00:30:26.970
So, in Darker, at the mid point of the book,
remember, Christian has a major miraculous

00:30:26.970 --> 00:30:32.999
breakthrough where he no longer feels compelled
to take out his psychosexual hangups on Ana,

00:30:32.999 --> 00:30:37.929
at least as far as physical violence is concerned
and he lets Ana touch him.

00:30:37.929 --> 00:30:44.309
So after that the big hangups would be his
need for petty control and his fear of parenthood

00:30:44.309 --> 00:30:46.770
which is tied in with his resentment of his
mother.

00:30:46.770 --> 00:30:51.879
Alright, in this book he resolves one of these,
which is that he gets over his very specific

00:30:51.879 --> 00:30:56.749
hangups about being afraid the baby will split
Ana’s attention and she won’t be able

00:30:56.749 --> 00:31:01.570
to devote all her energy to managing his emotions
anymore.

00:31:01.570 --> 00:31:06.499
But he’s still petty and controlling. Just
look at the whole email argument and the sunbathing

00:31:06.499 --> 00:31:09.740
argument and the went-out-drinking-with-Kate
argument.

00:31:09.740 --> 00:31:16.419
None of that comes back, none of it is resolved,
and Christian never lets go of that problem.

00:31:16.419 --> 00:31:19.440
But it’s the other one that’s just baffling.

00:31:19.440 --> 00:31:23.181
The first time Christian refers to his mother
in Fifty Shades of Grey he calls her a crack

00:31:23.181 --> 00:31:28.020
whore, and the last time he refers to her
in Fifty Shades Freed he calls her

00:31:28.020 --> 00:31:29.909
the crack whore.

00:31:29.909 --> 00:31:33.049
“How do you feel about that?” I whisper.

00:31:33.049 --> 00:31:38.049
He frowns. “About having no living relatives?
Fuck that if they were anything like the crack

00:31:38.049 --> 00:31:40.659
whore…” he shakes his head in disgust.

00:31:40.659 --> 00:31:45.820
So why is it that the film version is able
to see such an obvious bit of closure, while

00:31:45.820 --> 00:31:48.090
the originals weren’t?

00:31:48.090 --> 00:31:52.530
The answer is that Erika Mitchell just doesn’t
think that there’s anything wrong with this

00:31:52.530 --> 00:31:59.289
framing, since Ana also refers to Christian’s
mother as The Crack Whore.

00:31:59.289 --> 00:32:04.179
This is confusing – I’m his wife, not
his submissive, not some company he’s acquired.

00:32:04.179 --> 00:32:06.179
I’m not the crack whore who was his mother.

00:32:06.179 --> 00:32:10.979
Change of pace, let’s disassemble Jack’s
plot in its entirety, and for that we’re

00:32:10.979 --> 00:32:14.389
going to go all the way back to childhood.

00:32:14.389 --> 00:32:18.509
Jack Hyde spends his youth in the foster care
system of Michigan. When he is eight or nine

00:32:18.509 --> 00:32:23.090
Christian is put into the same home as him
for two months. At some point he is adopted

00:32:23.090 --> 00:32:28.190
or simply ages out but either way he’s able
to attend Princeton, graduate, and move out

00:32:28.190 --> 00:32:33.080
to Seattle where he gets a job as the fiction
editor at a successful boutique publisher.

00:32:33.080 --> 00:32:37.399
He also becomes a sex pervert and rapist who
molests his coworkers and keeps a log of sex

00:32:37.399 --> 00:32:39.070
tapes as leverage.

00:32:39.070 --> 00:32:44.869
Then one day he hires a new assistant named
Anastasia Steel. At the end of her first week

00:32:44.869 --> 00:32:50.509
on the job they go out to a bar where he meets
her boyfriend, Christian Grey, which starts

00:32:50.509 --> 00:32:53.210
some wheels spinning in Jack's head.

00:32:53.210 --> 00:32:58.889
The next night he infiltrates an exclusive
charity dinner and auction in order to creep on Christian.

00:32:58.889 --> 00:33:04.120
The following Tuesday he pins Anastasia against
the door of his office and gropes her, then

00:33:04.120 --> 00:33:08.799
gets kneed in the balls and beaten up by her
boyfriend’s security guard. Later that evening

00:33:08.799 --> 00:33:12.869
he’s fired because Christian has all but
officially bought the company.

00:33:12.869 --> 00:33:18.220
Two days later he sabotages Christian’s
helicopter Charlie Tango, causing it to crash

00:33:18.220 --> 00:33:24.289
in the forest near Mount Saint Helens, but
Christian survives. The next night Jack watches

00:33:24.289 --> 00:33:28.190
them celebrate Christian’s birthday from
across the lake at their home.

00:33:28.190 --> 00:33:32.429
Two months later he breaks into Christian
Grey’s offices and hacks into Grey’s personal

00:33:32.429 --> 00:33:34.830
files before firebombing the servers.

00:33:34.830 --> 00:33:41.369
He then contacts Liz and uses the threat of
releasing their sex tape to have her get into

00:33:41.369 --> 00:33:47.320
a car chase with Christian and Ana from the
countryside into downtown Seattle.

00:33:47.320 --> 00:33:52.659
What the point of this was remains unknown
because Ana manages to shake her off in traffic.

00:33:52.659 --> 00:33:57.710
A week and a half after the bombing he steals
an elevator maintenance van, outfits it with

00:33:57.710 --> 00:34:03.879
fake license plates, and uses the van and
I guess the tools in it to gain access to

00:34:03.879 --> 00:34:09.110
the elevator at Escala which gives him access
to the apartment where he hides in the closet

00:34:09.110 --> 00:34:14.230
with a kitchen knife until Ana comes home.
He then tries to kidnap Ana at knife point,

00:34:14.230 --> 00:34:19.020
but is incapacitated by her personal security
and arrested by police, who find a note in

00:34:19.020 --> 00:34:21.909
the van that says “you owe me a life”.

00:34:21.909 --> 00:34:28.490
A few weeks later he is granted bail set at
five hundred thousand dollars, which he pays.

00:34:28.490 --> 00:34:33.539
The next day Jack is released from custody
and with the help of Liz immediately kidnaps

00:34:33.539 --> 00:34:38.470
Christian’s sister Mia and tries to ransom
her for five million dollars, but is thwarted

00:34:38.470 --> 00:34:44.149
when Ana shoots him in the leg with a gun
and also the cops arrive and arrest him.

00:34:44.149 --> 00:34:46.230
So.

00:34:46.230 --> 00:34:51.169
It's maybe a little weird and pointless to
interrogate the inner life of a one-dimensional

00:34:51.169 --> 00:34:56.819
character, though it's just as weird that
Jack starts as Ana's antagonist, but we're

00:34:56.819 --> 00:35:02.780
assured that, no, he actually already hated
Christian before Christian got him fired,

00:35:02.780 --> 00:35:06.809
and everything with Ana is basically pure
coincidence.

00:35:06.809 --> 00:35:12.130
This is why the baby plotline feels so weird:
it doesn’t really matter because Ana doesn’t

00:35:12.130 --> 00:35:17.710
really want anything. The last thing she wanted
was a career of her own, so Christian bought

00:35:17.710 --> 00:35:22.460
her a publishing company. Since then she’s
pretty much been on auto-pilot.

00:35:22.460 --> 00:35:26.810
Part of what makes a thriller great is watching
a convoluted plan come together. A good thriller

00:35:26.810 --> 00:35:31.240
has a plan that’s complicated and maybe
even a little bit wacky but there’s this

00:35:31.240 --> 00:35:36.350
deep satisfaction from watching it all fall
into place.

00:35:36.350 --> 00:35:39.170
This… is not that.

00:35:39.170 --> 00:35:45.030
Jack’s scheme is less of a plan and more
of a series of disconnected twists that made

00:35:45.030 --> 00:35:51.039
good cliffhangers for a serial fanfiction
that was being written with an end goal but no plan.

00:35:51.039 --> 00:35:56.400
The longer you think about it the worse it
gets. I mean, for one all the individual parts

00:35:56.400 --> 00:36:01.650
are just absurd on their own merits. A woman
who works in human resources gets into a high

00:36:01.650 --> 00:36:07.770
speed chase through traffic for no discernible
reason. A fiction editor sabotages a private

00:36:07.770 --> 00:36:15.320
helicopter and firebombs a server after stealing
personal files that have no purpose or value

00:36:15.320 --> 00:36:16.500
in their own right.

00:36:16.500 --> 00:36:20.359
Now, if you want to talk about one of the
clearest examples of back-fill, where a writer

00:36:20.359 --> 00:36:25.520
has addressed a problem in their writing not
by going back and making changes to the earlier

00:36:25.520 --> 00:36:31.789
parts of the text but by explaining things
after the fact, we need to talk about Linc.

00:36:31.789 --> 00:36:36.630
The ultimate antagonist of the Fifty Shades
books, the puppet master at the middle of

00:36:36.630 --> 00:36:41.540
all of it, is a man named Linc, or Mister
Lincoln. He’s only mentioned in the books

00:36:41.540 --> 00:36:46.930
about five times total, depending on how you
count, and even the fan wiki considers him

00:36:46.930 --> 00:36:53.460
so superfluous to the story that he isn’t
even given an entry in the list of minor characters.

00:36:53.460 --> 00:36:58.559
An added scene at the end of the unrated cut
of the film refers to him as John Lincoln,

00:36:58.559 --> 00:37:06.310
so the movies eventually gave him a first
name, but he’s not in the theatrical cut at all.

00:37:06.310 --> 00:37:11.049
Even in the extended version he’s only in
two scenes in Freed, one where he’s introduced

00:37:11.049 --> 00:37:13.079
and one where he’s arrested.

00:37:13.079 --> 00:37:18.549
And you know what, I’m hard pressed to decide
which is worse storytelling: having an epilogue

00:37:18.549 --> 00:37:25.049
that amounts to “oh yeah, this guy, uh,
this guy here, he totally did it, he did it

00:37:25.049 --> 00:37:31.910
all, because… uh… because reasons. And
also he did it by funding a guy who wanted

00:37:31.910 --> 00:37:35.569
to destroy Christian’s life for completely
unrelated reasons.”

00:37:35.569 --> 00:37:41.200
Or having an epilogue that leaves you with
the absurd conclusion that a fiction editor

00:37:41.200 --> 00:37:46.430
would have the means, skill, and opportunity
to sabotage a helicopter in an incredibly

00:37:46.430 --> 00:37:51.720
precise way such that it would still work
but would fail after several hours of flight,

00:37:51.720 --> 00:37:55.690
infiltrate the high security areas of an office
tower belonging to a notoriously paranoid

00:37:55.690 --> 00:38:01.610
billionaire to plant a James Bond style bomb,
steal a car, forge a fake licence plate, break

00:38:01.610 --> 00:38:06.520
into the home of aforementioned billionaire
at a point when he has explicitly raised security,

00:38:06.520 --> 00:38:10.109
and pay half a million dollars in bail.

00:38:10.109 --> 00:38:15.440
If you think I’m being a little thin with
motivation here, this is about all that exists.

00:38:15.440 --> 00:38:21.079
Basically Linc found out about Christian’s
relationship with Elena and they got a divorce.

00:38:21.079 --> 00:38:25.770
But also he was abusing her, so they were
probably going to get a divorce anyway. Oh,

00:38:25.770 --> 00:38:31.010
look, there’s that thread again where people
become doms because of unresolved trauma,

00:38:31.010 --> 00:38:32.010
joy.

00:38:32.010 --> 00:38:38.210
But that’s it. That is the sum total of
Linc as he appears in both book and film.

00:38:38.210 --> 00:38:42.750
And, again, the only reason that the books
have a secret puppet master villain in the

00:38:42.750 --> 00:38:48.470
first place is because Mitchell needed to
explain how Jack suddenly had the resources

00:38:48.470 --> 00:38:53.940
to both get and pay bail when he’s been
charged with arson, grand theft auto, breaking

00:38:53.940 --> 00:38:56.760
and entering, AND attempted kidnapping.

00:38:56.760 --> 00:39:00.890
The funny thing is that this was really easy
to fix.

00:39:00.890 --> 00:39:03.260
Just make Elena the actual villain.

00:39:03.260 --> 00:39:08.670
From a filmmaking perspective this movie just
makes the previous movie all the more confusing,

00:39:08.670 --> 00:39:13.309
because it’s not like they were adapting
Fifty Shades Darker before Fifty Shades Freed

00:39:13.309 --> 00:39:14.319
was written.

00:39:14.319 --> 00:39:15.619
The books were done.

00:39:15.619 --> 00:39:20.099
You could read the whole thing and know how
it ends and work towards a conclusion instead

00:39:20.099 --> 00:39:25.700
of ending Fifty Shades Darker on the same
abrupt I-have-no-idea-where-I’m-going-with-this

00:39:25.700 --> 00:39:30.480
note that Mitchell did when she finished Master
of the Universe in 2010.

00:39:30.480 --> 00:39:36.490
I don’t know why they didn’t make that
change, why they decided to try and do a literal

00:39:36.490 --> 00:39:41.079
adaptation and then cut it for something just
as bad.

00:39:41.079 --> 00:39:48.710
The only answer I can posit is that reading
the books, the books are very defensive of Elena.

00:39:48.710 --> 00:39:52.840
So, keep in mind that Christian’s framing
of problems and conflicts is almost always

00:39:52.840 --> 00:39:56.859
taken as true or superior or justified.

00:39:56.859 --> 00:40:00.270
Christian consistently goes to bat for Elena.

00:40:00.270 --> 00:40:05.440
Whenever Ana does get to voice some criticism
of her Christian always defends Elena, and

00:40:05.440 --> 00:40:11.060
the final landing point in the book is Christian
saying that maybe a sexual relationship between

00:40:11.060 --> 00:40:16.920
a middle aged woman and a fifteen year old
boy isn’t a great idea, but they were both

00:40:16.920 --> 00:40:21.090
wrong, and they’d still be friends but Ana
doesn’t like Elena so he’s willing to

00:40:21.090 --> 00:40:22.660
let that friendship go.

00:40:22.660 --> 00:40:24.470
It’s messed up.

00:40:24.470 --> 00:40:31.190
In conclusion, Fifty Shades of Grey is a land
of contrasts, or as the natives called it:

00:40:31.190 --> 00:40:32.550
maize.

00:40:32.550 --> 00:40:36.890
It’s corny, is what I’m saying.

00:40:36.890 --> 00:40:41.700
The books are, and remain a contentious piece
of literature for the way they muddy the line

00:40:41.700 --> 00:40:48.600
between kink and abuse, replicating and reinforcing
abusive and potentially deadly relationship dynamics.

00:40:48.600 --> 00:40:53.359
The movies are a nominal thematic improvement,
but despite good changes to characterization

00:40:53.359 --> 00:40:57.859
and some light plot restructuring, the movies
ultimately hew too close to the books and

00:40:57.859 --> 00:41:01.710
in turn inherit almost all the original structural
problems.

00:41:01.710 --> 00:41:05.900
While the first movie shows a surprising amount
of creative talent and effort, the second

00:41:05.900 --> 00:41:10.750
and third are notably devoid of creative energy,
with long stretches where the lead actors

00:41:10.750 --> 00:41:13.790
seem irritated to still be there.

00:41:13.790 --> 00:41:19.390
Anyway, the movie denouements with a montage,
which is up there with dance number in the

00:41:19.390 --> 00:41:24.500
pantheon of “we didn’t know quite how
to end this one” but really the critical

00:41:24.500 --> 00:41:30.770
thing is that Twilight Saga Breaking Dawn
Part 2 ended with a little musical montage,

00:41:30.770 --> 00:41:35.510
so Fifty Shades has to end with a musical
montage.

00:41:35.510 --> 00:41:37.270
To quote fellow critic Jenny Nicholson

00:41:37.270 --> 00:41:43.150
“At least in Twilight those movies escalated
to an insane degree, so it was like “look

00:41:43.150 --> 00:41:48.210
how far they’ve come!” In this one they’re
just married now. It’s the same.”

00:41:48.210 --> 00:41:53.210
But you know what, I’m going to disagree
with that. Look at how far they’ve come.

00:41:53.210 --> 00:41:55.800
Look at how far we’ve all come.

00:41:55.800 --> 00:41:58.520
♫ I'm his wife, not his sub ♫

00:41:58.520 --> 00:42:03.520
♫ Not some company he's acquired ♫

00:42:03.520 --> 00:42:11.920
♫ I'm not the crack whore who was his mother ♫

00:42:12.960 --> 00:42:21.360
♫ You know, you're topping from the bottom ♫

00:42:21.520 --> 00:42:27.120
♫ He murmurs against my lips ♫

00:42:28.640 --> 00:42:36.560
♫ I don't understand what he's talking about ♫

00:42:37.760 --> 00:42:48.880
♫ Charlie Tango, 
she's a Eurocopter EC135 ♫

00:42:49.360 --> 00:43:00.880
♫ The safest in its class ♫

00:43:01.760 --> 00:43:04.240
♫ Dios mio! ♫

00:43:04.240 --> 00:43:08.049
♫ Double crap, me and my two left feet! ♫

00:43:08.049 --> 00:43:12.810
Now, there is one part of the whole fanfiction
and books that I’ve only incidentally mentioned

00:43:12.810 --> 00:43:17.289
up to this point, and that’s the straight
rip off of Midnight Sun, which was the version

00:43:17.289 --> 00:43:21.980
of Twilight told from Edward’s perspective
that Stephenie Meyer scrapped in 2008 after

00:43:21.980 --> 00:43:24.339
some test chapters were leaked to the internet.

00:43:24.339 --> 00:43:28.740
During the writing of Master of the Universe
Mitchell included several side chapters as

00:43:28.740 --> 00:43:33.720
bonus content on her website, including a
few that were just parallels of existing chapters

00:43:33.720 --> 00:43:35.770
told from Edward’s point of view.

00:43:35.770 --> 00:43:39.650
One of these was included at the end of Fifty
Shades Freed, and it’s basically chapters

00:43:39.650 --> 00:43:43.650
2 and 3 of Fifty Shades of Grey, but from
Christian’s perspective.

00:43:43.650 --> 00:43:49.630
In June 2015 Grey: Fifty Shades of Grey from
Christian’s Perspective was released.

00:43:49.630 --> 00:43:53.440
Now, this actually poses as an interesting
opportunity, especially since the book was

00:43:53.440 --> 00:43:58.829
being developed in parallel to the first film,
and released only a few months afterwards.

00:43:58.829 --> 00:44:02.700
Conceptually re-telling the story from Christian’s
point of view provides an opportunity to address

00:44:02.700 --> 00:44:07.430
and incorporate criticisms, maybe even going
as far as straight up retconning elements

00:44:07.430 --> 00:44:09.030
that didn’t work.

00:44:09.030 --> 00:44:13.730
Most interestingly, since it is now Christian
doing the telling it’s potentially a chance

00:44:13.730 --> 00:44:18.670
to re-characterize Christian via his internal
monologue, maybe bring him more in-line with

00:44:18.670 --> 00:44:22.160
Jamie Dornan’s performance of the character
rather than how the character would appear

00:44:22.160 --> 00:44:24.980
from a strict reading of the originals.

00:44:24.980 --> 00:44:29.270
It's essentially a third chance to adapt her
own story.

00:44:29.270 --> 00:44:35.220
I mean, surely it would be comical if a movie
came out and was commended for trying to make

00:44:35.220 --> 00:44:40.790
the abusive monster of Christian Grey into
someone at least conceptually likeable, and

00:44:40.790 --> 00:44:45.900
then the next book just doubled down on all
of Christian’s worst behaviours, indicating

00:44:45.900 --> 00:44:50.410
not just compulsion in his actions but malice
and forethought, right?

00:44:50.410 --> 00:44:54.800
Clearly, sensibly, Erika Mitchell is going
to seize this opportunity to paint a picture

00:44:54.800 --> 00:45:00.329
of a character who is in conflict with his
own evil, and isn’t just an abuser and rapist.

00:45:00.329 --> 00:45:07.130
“I’m not knowledgeable, Anastasia, I just
know what I like.” And I like you. “Some

00:45:07.130 --> 00:45:12.020
more?” I nod towards the bottle on the table.
“Please.”

00:45:12.020 --> 00:45:19.289
I fetch the champagne and refill her cup.
She regards me suspiciously. She knows I’m

00:45:19.289 --> 00:45:34.640
plying her with alcohol.

00:45:34.640 --> 00:45:49.400
Nope!

00:45:49.400 --> 00:46:18.920
[♫Charlie Tango♫]

